On Feb 13, 3:32 am, Patric Mueller <bh...@bigfoot.com> wrote:
> There is never ever a reason for a top quote.

I am not convinced that it is ever completely optimal.
Nonetheless, its proponents are correct in pointing out
that it is preferable to certain other behaviors that are
indeed worse -- e.g., quoting the entire other message,
consisting of multiple paragraphs discussing several
issues, then, at the bottom, appending a short reply
that addresses only one of them (and often it's not
entirely obvious which) or, in extreme cases, just
appending your signature at the bottom and hitting
the send button without actually writing anything.
Top-posting is very much preferable to that.

People who can handle figuring out which parts
of the original message are relevant to each point
they are making, so as to be able to quote relevant
excerpts and respond to them, should do so.

However, folks who are going to just quote the
whole original message every single time and direct
their reply at the thread in general, without taking the
time to structure their thoughts, edit their reply, and
respond point-for-point like an intelligent person would
do, had better set their newsreader to top-post mode.
It's the lesser evil.

